# =============================================================
# Operations — what THIS deployment does
# =============================================================
# Copy to `operations.yml` and describe your own work. Optional: a deployment
# that declares nothing behaves exactly as before.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# The framework decides the CLASSES OF CONSEQUENCE — reversible, undoable,
# outward-facing, irreversible — and what autonomy each class can earn. It
# deliberately does NOT decide which of your operations fall in them, because
# that is a fact about your work, not about the machine. Anything the framework
# cannot name classifies as unclassified, and unclassified is permanently
# propose-only: without this file, an operator whose work the shipped
# vocabulary happens not to describe can never move the autonomy ladder at all,
# and the always-gated ceilings guard nothing that exists in their world.
#
# EACH ROW
#   id          `<namespace>/<operation>` — exactly one "/", lowercase. The
#               namespace keeps your vocabulary from ever colliding with the
#               framework's own, so a declaration can never redefine a built-in.
#   invoked_as  the bare program or tool name a call arrives as. Matched
#               whole-token and case-insensitively, never as a substring, and
#               an absolute path resolves to the same bare name.
#   risk_class  which class of consequence this operation is. Run
#               `python3.12 -c "from framework.authority.matrix import
#               RISK_CLASSES; print(sorted(RISK_CLASSES))"` for the list.
#
# HOW IT FAILS
# Every failure mode narrows autonomy, never widens it. A malformed row is
# dropped, not repaired; a class this deployment's floor does not have is
# dropped; an unreadable file behaves as an empty one. A dropped operation
# stays unclassified, which is propose-only. A declaration is also consulted
# only AFTER every built-in ceiling rule, so it can neither escape a ceiling
# nor claim one it did not earn.
#
# CHOOSING THE CLASS — the only question that matters:
#   * Can it be put back automatically, with no one else involved?  reversible
#   * Can it be put back, but only by an explicit undo step?        …_write
#   * Does it reach a person, money, credentials or the live world
#     your customers depend on?                                     a ceiling
# When in doubt choose the stricter one: too strict costs a confirmation, too
# loose costs the thing that could not be put back.
# =============================================================

operations:
  # Something routine and self-contained: it changes only your own working
  # state, and putting it back is another run of the same kind of command.
  - id: example/routine-local-change
    invoked_as: [example-adjust]
    risk_class: reversible

  # Something that leaves a record other people work from, where "undo" is a
  # real, explicit step rather than an automatic one.
  - id: example/recorded-change
    invoked_as: [example-record, example-record-batch]
    risk_class: pm_write

  # Something irreversible in the world outside this machine. A ceiling row is
  # always gated regardless of how much confidence has been earned, so putting
  # your own irreversible operations here is what makes the ceiling protect
  # anything at all in YOUR deployment.
  - id: example/irreversible-release
    invoked_as: [example-release]
    risk_class: deploy_prod
